<section class="mb2-reading" aria-labelledby="mb2-reading-title">
  <header><h3 id="mb2-reading-title">Three danger points—and why MicroPath matters</h3><span id="mb2-scroll-hint">Scroll to read all three danger points ↕</span></header>
  <div class="mb2-reading-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="mb2-reading-title" aria-describedby="mb2-scroll-hint">
    <h4>Danger point one: the first bend</h4>
    <p>Finding MB two is only the beginning. Beyond the pulp chamber, the canal may curve abruptly. A hand file can enter, then stop, but resistance does not always mean the canal is blocked.</p>
    <p>A stiff stainless-steel file may press into the dentine instead of following the curve. Forcing it risks creating a ledge that makes further negotiation difficult. Moving from size ten to size fifteen increases tip diameter by fifty percent and reduces flexibility.</p>
    <p>Use small, appropriately pre-curved hand files for exploration. Respect the length they can negotiate without force, and reassess if progress stops.</p>
    <p>The seventeen-millimetre MicroPath MB two file is designed for this first danger point. Its nickel-titanium Flex-Ion construction provides flexibility for abrupt curves within the first three to five millimetres. Its short format creates handpiece headroom, while the size fifteen tip and point-zero-five taper provide an initial preparation step. The aim is controlled entry along the anatomy without creating a ledge.</p>
    <h4>Danger point two: the deeper curve</h4>
    <p>Passing the first bend does not mean the canal is straight. Deeper in the root, it may change direction again where the mesio-buccal one and mesio-buccal two canals meet. A hand file may pass the first curve but meet resistance here. Continued pressure can create a second ledge, sometimes obscured on a bite-wing radiograph.</p>
    <p>MicroPath size thirteen and fifteen glide-path files are designed for this deeper pathway. Both have a point-zero-three taper. Size thirteen provides an intermediate step between ten and fifteen; size fifteen develops the glide path further when anatomy permits. Their smaller taper limits the increase in diameter along the working portion.</p>
    <p>This creates a controlled transition to a reproducible glide path before larger shaping files are introduced. These files support careful exploration; they do not replace it. Let anatomy and tactile feedback guide each step.</p>
    <h4>Danger point three: instrument stress</h4>
    <p>At a deeper curve, a rotating instrument undergoes repeated tension and compression. Cyclic fatigue may cause separation, leaving a fragment toward the apex. Binding can also create torsional overload.</p>
    <p>MicroPath establishes the glide path before larger shaping files encounter this anatomy. The short MB two file manages initial access, followed by a size thirteen or fifteen glide-path file as appropriate. Each instrument has a defined role, rather than forcing one file through every stage. Flex-Ion technology is designed to add safety in this demanding zone.</p>
    <p>A University of Pennsylvania study by Iqbal, Kohli and Kim recorded eighty-one separated instruments in four thousand eight hundred and sixty-five endodontic cases. Almost eighty-three percent occurred in the apical third.</p>
    <p>MicroPath supports a safer sequence: establish entry, develop a confirmed glide path, then shape while respecting the curvature.</p>
  </div>
